      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Since $|x+1| = |(x+1)| \\geq 0$ and $|x-1| = |(x-1) | \\geq 0$ for any $x$, in order for the expression $|x+1| -|x-1|$ to be finite for every $x$, we require that only one of $(x+1) \\geq 0$ and $(x-1) \\geq 0$ is true for any value of x. Therefore, the right choice is (D).\nThe range of $|x+1| -|x-1|$ is [0,2).\nTo find the range of the function $G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|$, we need to analyze the behavior of the absolute value function for different cases within the domain.\n\n1. If x >= 1, then $(x + 1) \\geq 0$ and $(x - 1) \\geq 0$, so $G(x) = (x + 1) - |x - 1|$.\nIn this case, $G(x) = (x + 1) - (x - 1) = 2$, which is constant.\n\n2. If x < -1, then $(x + 1) < 0$ and $(x - 1) < 0$, so $G(x) = (x + 1) - (-(x - 1)) = (x + 1) + (x - 1) = 2x$.\nIn this case, $G(x) = 2x$ is a decreasing linear function, so the range is from $-2$ to $\\infty$ (i.e., $[-2, \\infty)$).\n\n3. If -1 <= x < 1, then $(x + 1) \\geq 0$ and $(x - 1) < 0$, so $G(x) = (x + 1) - (-(x - 1)) = (x + 1) + (x - 1) = 2x$.\nIn this case, $G(x) = 2x$ is an increasing linear function, so the range is from $-2$ to $2$ (i.e., $[-2, 2]$).\n\n4. If x >= 1, then $(x + 1) \\geq 0$ and $(x - 1) < 0$, so $G(x) = (-(x + 1)) - |x - 1|$.\nIn this case, $G(x) = -(x + 1) - (-(x - 1)) = -(x + 1) + (x - 1) = -2$, which is constant.\n\nNotice that the range of $|x+1| - |x-1|$ is $[-2,2]$, so the answer is (D).",
