      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to analyze the function $G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|$ and determine the range of $x$. We will do this by breaking the real number line into three separate intervals: $(-\\infty, -1)$, $[-1, 1]$, and $(1, \\infty)$.\n\nLet's examine $G(x)$ more closely in each of these intervals:\n\n1. For $x < -1$, we have $|x+1| = -(x+1)$ and $|x-1| = -(x-1)$. So, the function becomes $G(x) = -(x+1) - (-(x-1)) = -x-1+x-1 = -2$.\n2. For $x \\in [-1, 1]$, we consider two cases:\n   - If $-1 \\leq x < 0$, then $|x+1| = x+1$ and $|x-1| = -(x-1)$, so $G(x) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x$.\n   - If $x \\geq 0$, then $|x+1| = x+1$ and $|x-1| = x-1$, so $G(x) = x+1 - (x-1) = 2$.\n3. For $x > 1$, we have $|x+1| = x+1$ and $|x-1| = x-1$. So, the function becomes $G(x) = x+1 - (x-1) = 2$.\n\nSo, the function $G(x)$ can be expressed as:\n- $G(x) = -2$ for $x < -1$\n- $G(x)$ has two cases, dependent on $x$: $G(x) = 2x$ for $-1 \\leq x < 0$ and $G(x) = 2$ for $0 \\leq x \\geq 1$\n\nSince $G(x)$ has the value $-2$ in three separate intervals, we don't include $-2$ in the output range.\n\nBased on this analysis, we conclude that the range of $G(x)$ is all values from $-2$ up to and including $2$, that is:\n\n$[-2, 2]$",
